opam-version: "2.0"
maintainer: "Boris D. <borisd@gmx.com>"
build: [
  ["sh" "./setup.sh" "%{prefix}%/bin"]
]
depends: ["ocaml"]
available: os = "macos"
synopsis: "C cross toolchain for 32-bit ARM iOS devices (armv6/armv7/armv7s)"
description: """
Configures a C cross toolchain for one 32-bit ARM iOS target. As with
conf-ios, the target is chosen at install time and recorded in the package;
reinstall it to switch to another one.

  SUBARCH=armv6 SYSROOT=/path/to/iPhoneOS4.2.sdk opam install conf-ios-arm32

  SUBARCH   armv6   iPhone, iPhone 3G, iPod touch 1G/2G      (required)
            armv7   iPhone 3GS/4, iPad 1/2, iPod touch 3G/4G
            armv7s  iPhone 5, iPad 4
  SYSROOT   path to the SDK; or give SDK=<version> (and PLATFORM, default
            iPhoneOS) to resolve it under xcode-select as conf-ios does
  VER       deployment target; defaults to the SDK's own version

Apple has not shipped an SDK with a 32-bit ARM slice for many years and one
cannot be downloaded automatically, which is why SYSROOT usually has to be
given as a path. Old SDKs are found inside the matching Xcode installer:
"Xcode 3.2.5 and iOS SDK 4.2" carries Packages/iPhoneSDK4_2.pkg, which
pkgutil --expand-full unpacks. SUBARCH is checked against the slices the SDK
actually provides, since armv6 was dropped after iOS 5 and armv7s only
arrived with iOS 6.

Records arch, subarch, platform, sysroot, ver, cflags, ldflags and triple as
conf-ios does, plus cc: a compiler driver, needed because not all of the
workarounds below can be expressed as flags.

The current Xcode toolchain still emits and links 32-bit ARM, so no
period-era compiler is needed. Three gaps are covered. Modern ld refuses a
deployment target below iOS 5, so binaries are linked at 5.0 and the load
command is stamped back down afterwards when a lower target was asked for.
Current compiler-rt has no 32-bit ARM slice, so on cores with no division
instruction -- everything before armv7s -- the builtins come from the SDK's
libgcc, whose path varies by SDK release and is located at configure time.
And clang's generic per-architecture default CPU is narrowed to the part
actually present in these devices.
"""
extra-files: [
